HST PDF: wheel scrolling and Ctrl+wheel zoom follow the wheel, touchpads and pinches.

Owner report (Windows, 1.0.1/1.0.2): in HST PDF the wheel scrolls slowly and lags, while dragging
the scrollbar is smooth. Writer, Sheets, Slides and Draw scroll through vcl's
Window::HandleScrollCommand and are not affected.

The viewer turned a wheel event into pixels as -GetDelta() / 120 * max(1, GetScrollLines()) * 18:
- a notch scrolled 54 pixels through an ease-out of a third of the remaining distance per 16 ms
  timer tick: 11 ticks, 225 ms measured under Xvfb, about 340 ms on Windows where a 16 ms timer
  fires on the 15.6 ms clock tick (31 ms), the last ticks one pixel each;
- GetDelta() is in platform units and GetScrollLines() already carries the part of a notch a
  touchpad or high-resolution wheel sent (vcl/win salframe: lines = delta / 120 * lines per notch),
  so small events were scaled down twice and then rounded per event: a precision touchpad gesture
  worth 3 notches (30 events of delta 12) scrolled 60 pixels instead of 162, a high-resolution
  wheel notch (8 events of delta 15) 16 instead of 54; on macOS (delta is a few points) a mouse
  notch scrolled 0 pixels.
Ctrl+wheel zoomed 10% per event whatever its size, so a touchpad pinch (many small Ctrl+wheel
events) jumped by 10% per event, and the zoom kept the top of the view instead of the pointer.

Scrolling: the distance is what vcl uses for every other window, notches times lines per notch, at
50/3 pixels a line (50 a notch at the usual three lines). What is below a pixel is kept for the
next event. A notch of two lines or more glides over a fixed 100 ms (ease-out cubic, 10 ms timer),
so it ends on time whatever the timer resolution; smaller steps (touchpads) and pixel deltas are
applied directly; a jump (zoom, search, page) ends a running glide. "One screen at a time" scrolls
nine tenths of the view, as Page Down does.

Zoom: a whole notch is still one 10% step, a part of a notch (delta / 120) zooms by the same part
of a step, and a macOS pinch (pixel delta = magnification x 125) by its magnification. The
document point under the pointer stays under it; the zoom stays within 10%..640% and a zoom that
does not change (at a limit) renders nothing again. The zoom box follows as before. Every zoom
rendered the visible pages again through PDFium (70-90 ms of CPU an event here), too slow for the
stream of events of a pinch: a wheel zoom now shows the pages scaled from their last rendering and
renders them once, 150 ms after the last event.
